  "Question": "What is the x-coordinate of the center of the circle \\(x^2+y^2-4x+2y-4=0\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(D=-4\\), so the x-coordinate of the center is \\(-D/2=2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Reveal the center in standard form\n\nCompleting the square makes the center coordinates visible in standard form. Since \\(x^2-4x=(x-2)^2-4\\) and \\(y^2+2y=(y+1)^2-1\\), the equation becomes\n\\[\n(x-2)^2+(y+1)^2=9.\n\\]\nIts center is \\((2,-1)\\), so the x-coordinate is \\(2\\).",
